Conclusions
In this work, we have performed a meta-analysis of phosphoproteomics datasets for rice, mapped onto the reference Nipponbare proteome. The pipeline includes conservative statistics to avoid reporting false positives, and a simple Gold-Silver-Bronze metric allowing users of the data to focus understand the likelihood of a site being correct.
The dataset has been deposited into UniProtKB enabling sites to be analysed alongside any other data held there about protein structure/function, including AlphaFold2 predictions for all rice proteins. The data is also available in PeptideAtlas and PRIDE, enabling detailed exploration of scores and visualization of source mass spectra, as a full evidence trail.
We have also mapped the data to variation coming from the 3,000 genome set, creating a resource for allele mining, where phosphosites are likely to have lost function due to amino acid substitutions in some rice varieties, with alterations to downstream cell signalling pathways. We expect this will be a powerful resource for rice biology, and all datasets are fully open and available for re-analysis.
